oo many posts down  the issue is that they're leaving too  many dangerous posts  up throughout december the president  continued to spread misinformation about  the election results  then on january 6th the capitol building  was attacked by a pro-trump mob  i wouldn't say this was a straw that  broke the camel's back i would say this  is the political tides that are turning  robin kaplan is a researcher at data and  society  whose work focuses on online  disinformation and moderation  data in society is an independent  nonprofit think tank  that received initial funding from  microsoft but today receives funding  from a number of sources what we're  seeing right now is  an outgoing administration and many of  the platforms saying okay well we were  probably going to take this action  anyway because we had kind of  constructed a system of rules where  um trump as a major figure was held to a  different set of standards  come january 20th he was going to be  held to the same standard so he would  have been  most likely kicked off anyway now that  big tech has effectively banished trump  and some of his followers the big  question is where will they go  shortly after the bans some of his  supporters tried to migrate to a small  social network called parlor but then  its apps were removed from the apple  and google app stores the same day  amazon announced  it would eject parlor from its web  hosting service  we're going to be back online one day  and hopefully soon  as soon as possible but um this is a  real challenge we have to build our own  infrastructure our own  everything in order to do it parlor is  one  of a handful of up-and-coming so-called  alt-tech platforms that include gab and  aikoon  which was previously known as a-chan  these sites brand themselves as bastions  of free speech  in an increasingly restrictive internet  but critics claim  these sites are havens for hate speech  in a statement to the wall street  journal  gab ceo andrew torba wrote hate speech  is protected by the first amendment  and therefore it is protected on gab  thank you very much  the legal foundation for free speech on  the internet is section 230.  today with the stroke of a pen our laws  will catch up  with our future a tersely worded statute  tucked into the 1996 communications  decency act  section 230 shields tech companies from  liability  related to the content that's posted  onto their sites basically it means that  their platforms they bring people  together  but they're not responsible they can't  be held legally liable  for different types of threats or other  content that are posted to them  some believe that the best solutions lie  in legislative reform of section 230  but both trump and biden have called for  230 to be repealed  we're putting a lot on social media  companies and i do think that social  media  companies have a responsibility to  moderate the content on their networks  but  what we're seeing is not something that  was necessarily borne out by  social media these are what we're what  we're seeing right now  is um tensions that reflect  very long-standing political divides in  america  what is clear is that there are no easy  answers when it comes to content  moderation  on january 13th twitter ceo jack dorsey  tweeted that he felt torn about his  company's decision  the day after president biden's  inauguration facebook said its internal  oversight board  would review the company's decision to  indefinitely suspend former president  trump
 

